From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:20:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/11] clutter/color: Bound the white adaptation with diagonal
 output factors

The white adaptation of a display's output adapted with Bradford in CIE
XYZ and normalized the result so that the adapted white kept its
brightest channel. That does not bound any other color: a Bradford
matrix mixes channels, and at 4000 K a saturated BT.2020 yellow comes
out with a red channel about 0.5% above its unadapted value, against
the contract that Night Light never brightens a channel.

Use diagonal von Kries factors in the linear RGB of the output's
encoding instead, the same per-channel family SDR Night Light scales the
gamma ramp with. The Planckian white is converted to that RGB through
XYZ without adaptation, a negative component is clamped to zero, and
the factors are divided by the largest. They are finite, in [0, 1], and
the largest is exactly 1, so no channel of a nonnegative color exceeds
its unadapted value. A white inside the encoding gamut, such as the
4000 K acceptance point, is shown at exactly its chromaticity; one
outside it, such as 1000 K in BT.2020, whose blue is negative, is
projected into nonnegative RGB rather than claimed exactly. The clamp
is parameter construction, not per-pixel clipping.

The matrix on linear BT.2020 is the conversion to the encoding's linear
RGB, the factors, and the conversion back, so the pipeline keeps its one
matrix uniform and a temperature step still keeps its shader.

Test the factors against an independent double precision computation
from 1000 K to 10000 K on BT.2020 and DCI-P3 outputs, the 1000 K
projection, the channel bound on primaries, secondaries and white, and
the exact chromaticity of representable whites on an output whose
white is not D65. Note where the clutter tests' Planckian constants
come from.
